 of vocabulary  going into Chinese which he wouldn't  have in Spanish and the fact that it's  easier to pronounce  some people are good at pronunciation  others aren't generally speaking people  who are good at pronouncing one second  language are good at pronouncing other  second languages so to me that is quite  misleading and it would suggest that a  Spanish person would have an easier time  learning Japanese which where the sounds  the vowel sounds at least are very  similar to Spanish then let's say French  where the vowel sounds are difficult  different well in fact that's not true a  Spanish person will learn French very  easily and will not learn Japanese very  easily he was on to say you know analyse  the grammatical structure whether it's  subject-verb-object  sv o or s ol v subject-object-verb  and that will determine if it's easy  well that's simply not so I mean German  is subject-object-verb  unlike English or Swedish whereas  Chinese is subject-verb-object  but a so each person will have an awful  lot easier time learning German than  learning Chinese you know yeah how  similar the languages aren't obviously  that's gonna help but then it was on to  suggest that that if you study both  Portuguese and Spanish  there will be fate whole he calls them  interference because they're similar  well that's not true there's all kinds  of people who speak three or four  Romance languages and speak them well or  speak two or three Slavic languages and  speak them well so that most of this  quite honestly is just nonsense and he  goes on in great detail about  complicated stuff about ik axillary  verbs and sounds and scripts he goes on  to suggest that a Spanish speaker would  have trouble learning Brazilian  Portuguese because the sounds are so  different well I can tell you as someone  who speaks Spanish and is learning  Portuguese that is simply not so  he says that anyway what it's it's all  nonsense but the interesting thing is  that not much earlier he had a