milar subject-verb-object structure  in the language if you are a few have a  spanish boyfriend or girlfriend or if  you love the culture of China or Japan  or Russia and if you really connect with  that language from an emotional  passionate point of view you will do  well in that language and some languages  are more difficult and it is certainly  true that the languages and the big  thing to look for is vocabulary the more  similar the vocabulary that easier it  will be because the big battle is  accumulating all the words you need in  order to be able to deal with different  situations and in my case for example  Chinese was very difficult for me  because all of our cabinet or it was new  although Chinese is an easy language to  learn in terms of vocabulary because  it's so systematic the different  components that characters fit together  to you know combined in a compound to  create a new word and very often you can  guess the meaning so but with Chinese  then going into Japanese which is a very  different language structurally it was a  tremendous advantage to me that I was  able to read the characters and I  therefore had an instant vocabulary of  50% of the words of Japanese now when I  went from Japanese to Korean which is  not a language that I don't yet speak  although I have studied it the fact that  its structure the order and the way they  instead of prepositions they have post  positions and so forth that helped me so  similarity in structure helps but  similarity in vocabulary is a much  bigger deal all right  so however Tim Ferriss I gotta hand it  to him I went out and bought his book  you know I kind of flipped through it  it's typical of these self-help books  you can flip through them pretty quickly  but there are some useful you know URLs  there or places to outsource in order to  simplify your life and I am gonna go  back there and do a little more reading  so there you have it my take on Tim  Ferriss
 

